{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-i2-reviewer-fable-42","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:6aac19a2a2aa18f85740","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","bounded_statement":null,"root_cause":null,"issue_class":null,"evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The declared independent unit is bog_id, and one row is one bog with a single flux reading, so the issue class would require some bog_id to contribute more than one row to the t-test.","observed_value":"unit column bog_id; one row = one bog with one reading","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":8,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"One row is: one surveyed peat bog together with its single midsummer methane flux reading\nIndependent unit column: bog_id"}]},{"description":"The workflow applies a row-independent procedure (pooled two-sample Student t-test with equal_var=True) directly to the per-row flux values, so each CSV row enters the test as one observation.","observed_value":"stats.ttest_ind(intact, drained, equal_var=True) on per-row ch4_flux_mg_m2_h values","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":60,"end_line":60,"quoted_text":"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(intact, drained, equal_var=True)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":31,"end_line":32,"quoted_text":"identifiers.append(record[UNIT_COLUMN].strip())\n            fluxes[label].append(float(record[FLUX_COLUMN]))"}]},{"description":"Recomputation from the raw CSV values reproduces the reported statistics: intact fluxes (3.20, 3.50, 2.90, 3.40, 3.00, 3.20) mean 3.200; drained fluxes (2.55, 2.95, 2.30, 3.10, 2.45, 2.85) mean 2.700; difference 0.500; t(10) = 3.1623, p = 0.01012 as reported.","observed_value":{"mean_intact":3.2,"mean_drained":2.7,"difference":0.5,"t":3.1623,"p":0.01012,"dof":10},"source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":25,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Intact bogs emitted more methane than drained bogs: mean flux 3.200 vs 2.700 mg m-2 h-1 (difference 0.500), pooled two-sample t-test t(10) = 3.1623, two-sided p = 0.01012, standardised effect size 1.826."},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":1,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"bog_id,catchment,hydrology,peat_depth_cm,ch4_flux_mg_m2_h\nPB-101,Coire Moss,intact,412,3.20\nPB-102,Ardvarn Flats,drained,388,2.55\nPB-103,Glen Rannoch,intact,455,3.50\nPB-104,Braemore Basin,drained,401,2.95\nPB-105,Sruth Bealach,intact,367,2.90\nPB-106,Tarn Machair,drained,352,2.30\nPB-107,Kilbrae Hollow,intact,498,3.40\nPB-108,Dunmore Levels,drained,437,3.10\nPB-109,Feorlin Mire,intact,421,3.00\nPB-110,Auchtree Carr,drained,396,2.45\nPB-111,Balloch Sike,intact,433,3.20\nPB-112,Ravenhaugh Fen,drained,410,2.85"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"All twelve data rows carry distinct bog_id values (PB-101 through PB-112) and twelve distinct catchment names, so no independent unit appears on more than one row and no bog enters the t-test more than once.","observed_value":{"rows":12,"distinct_bog_ids":12,"distinct_catchments":12},"source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"PB-101,Coire Moss,intact,412,3.20\nPB-102,Ardvarn Flats,drained,388,2.55\nPB-103,Glen Rannoch,intact,455,3.50\nPB-104,Braemore Basin,drained,401,2.95\nPB-105,Sruth Bealach,intact,367,2.90\nPB-106,Tarn Machair,drained,352,2.30\nPB-107,Kilbrae Hollow,intact,498,3.40\nPB-108,Dunmore Levels,drained,437,3.10\nPB-109,Feorlin Mire,intact,421,3.00\nPB-110,Auchtree Carr,drained,396,2.45\nPB-111,Balloch Sike,intact,433,3.20\nPB-112,Ravenhaugh Fen,drained,410,2.85"}]},{"description":"The workflow actively enforces one row per independent unit: it raises a ValueError if any bog identifier is repeated, so repeated-unit entry into the t-test is prevented at runtime.","observed_value":"uniqueness guard on identifiers before analysis","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":35,"end_line":36,"quoted_text":"if len(set(identifiers)) != len(identifiers):\n        raise ValueError(\"a bog identifier is repeated; every row must be a separate bog\")"}]},{"description":"The data description and the generated report both attest that no bog was measured twice and that rows read equals distinct bog identifiers (12 = 12), consistent with what the CSV shows.","observed_value":"Rows read: 12; distinct bog identifiers: 12","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":5,"end_line":6,"quoted_text":"No bog was visited\ntwice, and no two rows describe the same bog, so the twelve rows are twelve separate sites."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":7,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"bog contributes exactly one closed-chamber flux measurement, so each row of data/input.csv is\none independent unit. Rows read: 12; distinct bog identifiers: 12."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to demonstrate the issue by searching the supplied files for any way a single independent unit could enter the t-test more than once: I checked every bog_id in data/input.csv for duplicates (all 12 are distinct), checked the catchment column for a hidden grouping that could make two rows the same unit (all 12 catchments are distinct, matching the description that each catchment holds exactly one bog), traced workflow/analysis.py to confirm each CSV row contributes exactly one value to exactly one group with no row duplication, resampling, or re-reading of the file, and verified the code would raise an error on any repeated identifier. I also recomputed the group means, difference, t-statistic, and p-value from the raw rows and matched them to the report, confirming the test used exactly the 12 unique-unit rows. No path to repeated-unit entry was found, so the falsification attempt failed and the no-issue verdict stands.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}